    "doi": "https://doi.org/10.1038/s41598-026-39887-5",
    "abstract": "The interplay between population growth and climate warming is reshaping human exposure to temperature extremes worldwide, yet how this composite effect varies across regions and income groups remains insufficiently quantified. Here, we examine whether global climate warming, in combination with population redistribution, is shifting the balance between heating and cooling demand, and how this transition varies across countries with different socioeconomic conditions. By integrating gridded population data with long-term changes in cooling and heating degree days, we assess population exposure to heat and cold at global and regional scales. Combining population distribution with degree-days changes, we find that the global balance of temperature-related demand is shifting from heating toward relatively greater cooling demand. Notably, low- and lower-middle-income countries, which have relatively limited resilience to heat stress, face greater cooling demand challenges and account for 63.65% of global cooling-related population exposure, which has increased by 31.24% over the past four decades. In contrast, high and upper-middle-income countries, which are more resilient, account for 90.69% of global heating-related population exposure, growing at a rate of 22.29%. Our findings highlight pronounced inequalities in population exposure to temperature extremes, provide a global assessment of the shifting balance between heating and cooling demand, and offer insights relevant to climate adaptation and public health policy development.",
    "field": "Climate change impacts",
    "problem": "How the combined effect of population growth and climate warming on heating and cooling demand varies across regions and income groups remains insufficiently quantified, obscuring inequalities in population exposure to temperature extremes.",
    "method": "This paper proposes a method that integrates gridded population data with heating and cooling degree-days to quantify historical population exposure to temperature demands and decompose the change into climatic and demographic drivers globally.",
    "task": "The task is to analyze historical global population exposure to heating and cooling degree days by integrating population and climate data to quantify the shifting balance between heating and cooling demands.",
